WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:03.435
- [Instructor] So we're told
that Lynnette can wash 95 cars

00:03.435 --> 00:05.176
in five days.

00:05.176 --> 00:08.785
How many cars can
Lynnette wash in 11 days?

00:08.785 --> 00:10.217
So like always, pause this video

00:10.217 --> 00:12.755
and see if you can figure this out.

00:12.755 --> 00:14.785
So the way that I would like to tackle it

00:14.785 --> 00:18.952
is given the information they
gave us, 95 cars in five days,

00:20.309 --> 00:23.760
can we figure out how many
cars she can wash per day?

00:23.760 --> 00:25.867
How many cars in one day?

00:25.867 --> 00:27.755
And then we could just multiply that by 11

00:27.755 --> 00:30.516
to figure out how many
she could wash in 11 days.

00:30.516 --> 00:33.099
So there's 95 cars in five days

00:37.686 --> 00:39.115
and so instead of five,

00:39.115 --> 00:42.032
if we were to say one day, one day,

00:43.359 --> 00:46.583
well, to go from five days to
one day, we divide by five.

00:46.583 --> 00:48.869
So the number of cars
she can do in one day,

00:48.869 --> 00:50.745
that would be that divided by five.

00:50.745 --> 00:53.844
In either case, we would divide by five

00:53.844 --> 00:55.995
and what's 95 divided by five?

00:55.995 --> 00:57.596
You might recognize that well, look,

00:57.596 --> 01:00.045
five times 20 is equal to 100.

01:00.045 --> 01:02.462
So five times 19 is 95.

01:02.462 --> 01:06.460
So you might recognize
that's 19 cars in one day

01:06.460 --> 01:08.868
or if that math I just did
seems a little bit too fast,

01:08.868 --> 01:12.421
you could just take five into 95.

01:12.421 --> 01:15.366
Five goes into nine one
time, one times five is five,

01:15.366 --> 01:19.888
subtract, nine minus five is
four, bring down that five,

01:19.888 --> 01:23.969
five goes into 45 nine
times, nine times five is 45

01:23.969 --> 01:25.216
and it goes in perfectly.

01:25.216 --> 01:26.296
There's no remainder.

01:26.296 --> 01:29.432
So all I did is I divided
both of these numbers by five

01:29.432 --> 01:32.712
to figure out how many
she can wash per day.

01:32.712 --> 01:37.286
So she can wash 19 cars in
one day, 19 cars per day

01:37.286 --> 01:38.496
and so if I wanna figure out

01:38.496 --> 01:42.496
how many she can wash in
11 days, so in 11 days,

01:44.578 --> 01:47.637
well, now, I am just multiplying by 11.

01:47.637 --> 01:51.804
So if I multiply 19 times
11, what am I going to get?

01:53.898 --> 01:55.856
Well, if you're good at
the mental mathematics,

01:55.856 --> 01:59.490
you might recognize 19
times 10 would be 190

01:59.490 --> 02:01.737
and then you would have one more 19

02:01.737 --> 02:03.820
so that would be 209 cars

02:05.041 --> 02:09.061
or you could just multiply 19 times 11.

02:09.061 --> 02:12.978
One times 19 is 19 and
then one times 19 is 19.

02:14.293 --> 02:16.793
Add 'em together, you get 209.

02:19.029 --> 02:22.029
So she can wash 209 cars in 11 days.

02:23.554 --> 02:25.637
Let's do another example.

02:26.718 --> 02:30.885
Here, we are told at the market,
eight batteries cost $10.

02:32.592 --> 02:34.558
How much do six batteries cost?

02:34.558 --> 02:37.419
Once again, pause this video
and try to figure it out.

02:37.419 --> 02:39.388
Well, I'm gonna do the same technique.

02:39.388 --> 02:41.399
If eight batteries cost $10,

02:41.399 --> 02:44.182
let's figure out how
much one battery costs.

02:44.182 --> 02:46.349
So one battery costs what?

02:50.472 --> 02:51.493
Figure that out.

02:51.493 --> 02:53.931
Well, it's going to be 1/8th.

02:53.931 --> 02:56.374
To go from eight to
one, I divide by eight.

02:56.374 --> 02:59.112
So what happens when I divide 10 by eight?

02:59.112 --> 03:02.749
That's gonna be my per battery cost.

03:02.749 --> 03:06.916
So 10 divided by eight
is the same thing as 5/4

03:09.404 --> 03:12.545
which is the same thing as one and 1/4

03:12.545 --> 03:14.875
which is the same thing if
we're talking in terms of money,

03:14.875 --> 03:17.176
it might be more useful to write it like

03:17.176 --> 03:19.404
one and 25 hundredths

03:19.404 --> 03:21.654
or one battery costs $1.25.

03:24.632 --> 03:26.352
And so when they're saying six batteries,

03:26.352 --> 03:28.598
how much do six batteries cost,

03:28.598 --> 03:32.181
well, if we're going
to six batteries cost,

03:34.971 --> 03:37.997
we're multiplying the number
of batteries times six now.

03:37.997 --> 03:40.726
So what's $1.25 times six?

03:40.726 --> 03:44.109
So it's gonna be $6 plus another $1.50

03:44.109 --> 03:46.723
'cause six times 25 is 150.

03:46.723 --> 03:49.333
So it's gonna be $7.50

03:49.333 --> 03:51.297
and just to make sure the
math I just did was right,

03:51.297 --> 03:53.880
let's multiply $1.25 times six.

03:55.548 --> 03:57.424
Six times five is 30.

03:57.424 --> 04:00.811
Six times two is 12 plus three is 15.

04:00.811 --> 04:03.180
Six times one is six plus one is seven

04:03.180 --> 04:06.036
and we have a total of two
digits behind the decimal

04:06.036 --> 04:08.860
so there you have it, you have $7.50.

04:08.860 --> 04:11.839
Now, there's other ways
that you could tackle this.

04:11.839 --> 04:14.004
You could say that hey, this is,

04:14.004 --> 04:16.530
six batteries is gonna cost 6/8ths

04:16.530 --> 04:18.123
as much as eight batteries

04:18.123 --> 04:20.369
but that's a little bit
confusing, at least for my brain.

04:20.369 --> 04:23.063
I like to go just down to
the unit cost you could say,

04:23.063 --> 04:26.298
the cost per battery and
then multiply it by six.

04:26.298 --> 04:29.742
It gives me more comfort in
understanding what I'm doing.
